WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.950
WandaVision proves that you don't always necessarily
need to jump into the meat of a story.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:09.120
You can take your time, forcing the audience
to question your motives and speculate as

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.610
to where, precisely, you're heading.

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:15.360
That was the case with Episode 1 of the hit
Disney+ original, as the series followed a

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:17.680
pretty straightforward sitcom plot.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.000
With each passing episode, the audience receives
more information until the explosive final

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.410
scene in Episode 5 when Wanda and Vision hear
a knock at the door and discover it's Wanda's

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:28.890
brother, Pietro.

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:33.400
Only it's not the MCU Quicksilver played by
Aaron Taylor-Johnson who bit the dust in Age

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:38.870
of Ultron; it's Evan Peters, who played the
superhero in the 20th Century Studios X-Men

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:39.870
movies.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:43.360
We've known for a while that Peters would
make an appearance on WandaVision, resulting

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:47.450
in rampant fan theories that his presence
would open up the gates between the Marvel

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:51.780
Cinematic Universe and the larger Marvel multiverse,
which includes the X-Men.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:55.630
However, given his brief introduction, there's
still a lot we don't know.

00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:59.220
Is this actually the Quicksilver we last saw
in X-Men: Dark Phoenix?

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:02.160
Is this a different guy who happens to look
like Evan Peters?

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.620
And what did Darcy mean with this line?

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.410
"She recast Pietro?"

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:10.950
Things are only going to get crazier from
here as we get into the final four episodes.

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:16.150
Fortunately, show creator Jac Schaeffer sat
down with Marvel.com to discuss the phenomenal

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:19.380
casting coup and how exactly it came to be.

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:23.480
By the sound of it, Schaeffer knew early on
she wanted to try to bring Peters' Quicksilver

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:27.020
back, but naturally, that led to a fair share
of challenges.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:28.020
As she explains,

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:32.220
"That's the thing, you can hatch a million
great ideas, but to make them land, to make

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:35.659
them be grounded, to make them feel organic
to the larger story ..."

00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:39.240
This sentiment and the idea that his role
had to make sense in the overall structure

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:43.430
of the series was echoed by Marvel Studios
president Kevin Feige.

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:44.430
Schaeffer continued,

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:48.030
"Kevin wanted to make sure that there was
a reason for it, that it made sense.

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:49.620
And I hope that's what we did."

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:53.710
It's safe to say the mission was accomplished,
and given the fact that Vision actor Paul

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:58.260
Bettany has said that a massive cameo would
be coming down the line, Quicksilver's entrance

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:01.230
won't be the only thing that blows our mind
with this show.

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:05.650
It's such a trippy series that it would be
easy to go overboard with craziness, but grounding

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:09.470
things in some definition of reality helps
keep the show honest.

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:11.730
Schaeffer further expounds upon this, saying,

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:16.140
"This show is such a mind scramble, and because
it's working on so many levels, and there's

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:19.930
so many notions of what's real and what's
not, and performance, and casting, and audience,

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:24.470
and fandom, and all of that, we just thought
it would be the biggest thrill to bring Evan

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:27.170
over to the Marvel Cinematic Universe."

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:31.160
All questions regarding where, exactly, this
new Quicksilver came from and what his role

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:35.780
will be going forward will receive answers
eventually, and for the time being, we already

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:39.400
have a good idea of what his role in the sitcom
world will be.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:44.020
Darcy saying that Wanda "recast" her deceased
brother wasn't just a throwaway line.

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:48.170
Recasting characters, even prominent ones,
in sitcoms is a common trope that can be seen

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:49.750
time and time again.

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:54.120
Just take Bewitched, which replaced Dick York
with Dick Sargent as co-lead halfway through

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.120
the series.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:59.730
Of course, modern audiences may be more familiar
with Janet Hubert-Whitten departing the role

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:04.450
of Aunt Viv on The Fresh Prince of Bel-Air
after feuding with star Will Smith and getting

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.960
replaced with Daphne Maxwell Reid.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:11.050
Replacing Taylor-Johnson's Quicksilver with
Evans' version just makes sense.

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:16.090
WandaVision had to recast Pietro to further
solidify its sitcom influences, and it undoubtedly

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:19.140
sets up some bigger surprises in the weeks
to come.

